To simplify the presentation of the governing equations describing our GB solute drag model, we use vector and matrix notation in the following sections.

Thermodynamics of Ternary Alloys

We start with a substitutional ternary alloy of host element labeled A and solutes labeled B and C with concentration fields cA, cB, and cC, where cA+cB+cC = 1. Mass fluxes arise due to gradients in the chemical potentials and are given by Ji = - \sum j=A,B,C Lij \nabla\mu˜ j, i=A,B,C, where \mu˜ j is the chemical potential of species j and Lij are the Onsager transport coefficients. Using the Gibbs-Duhem equation, the flux equations for solutes B and C can be expressed respectively as JB = -LBB \nabla\muB - LBC \nabla\muC and JC = -LCB \nabla\muB - LCC \nabla\muC where \mu i = \mu˜ i - \mu˜ A is the chemical potential of species i relative to that of the host A, i.e., the diffusion potential. Considering that \mu B = \mu B(cB,cC) and \mu C = \mu C(cB,cC), we use the relation \nabla\mu B = (\partial\mu B / \partial cB) (\partial cB / \partial x) + (\partial\mu B / \partial cC) (\partial cC / \partial x) and a similar expression for \nabla\mu C to arrive at the following for mass fluxes

{ JB } = - [ LBB LBC ] { \partial\muB / \partial cB \partial\muB / \partial cC } { \nabla cB } = - [ DBB DBC ] { \nabla cB }
{ JC } [ LCB LCC ] { \partial\muC / \partial cB \partial\muC / \partial cC } { \nabla cC } [ DCB DCC ] { \nabla cC }

where Dij are the relevant diffusion coefficients. In this work, we assume bulk diffusion is frozen compared to the one in the GB and we write

Dij = Dgb ij \Upsilon(x). (11)

Assuming an ideal ternary alloy, where the chemical potential difference of the ith species is \mu i = kB T ln(ci/(1 - cB - cC)), one can arrive at analytical expressions for Dij given by

Dij = kB T ( Lij / ci - LiA / cA ), i,j=B,C. (12)

Derivation of the governing equations for solute drag

We consider a semi-infinite bicrystal ternary alloy of host element A and solutes B and C described using composition fields cA(x,t), cB(x,t) and cC(x,t), where x is the direction measured from the GB and t is time. An indicator function \Upsilon(x) given by Eq. 2 is used to interpolate bulk fb(c,T) and GB fgb(c,T) free energies. Here, x \in (- \infty, - \delta) and x \in (+ \delta, + \infty) define the spatial extent of grains 1 and 2, respectively, and the GB is defined in the spatial region x \in (- \delta, + \delta). Regular solution thermodynamics47 are used for the functional form of the bulk and GB free energies leading to expressions for the chemical potentials that are given by Eq. 3.

We express solute transport equations in a frame moving with the migrating GB at a velocity V, where the steady-state solute governing equations are described by

V { \partial cB / \partial x } = { \partial JB / \partial x }
{ \partial cC / \partial x } { \partial JC / \partial x }

Then, we integrate Eq. 13 above using the far-field concentration boundary condition for the ith solute ci = c\infty i as x \to \infty leading to

{ JB } = - [ LBB LBC ] { \nabla\mu B } = V { cB - c\infty B }
{ JC } [ LCB LCC ] { \nabla\mu C } { cC - c\infty C }

Next, using Eq. 3 we re-express gradients in the diffusion potential in terms of concentration gradients as

{ \nabla\mu B } = [ WBB WBC ] { \partial cB / \partial x } + \Upsilon' { UB }
{ \nabla\mu C } [ WCB WCC ] { \partial cC / \partial x } { UC }

where \Upsilon' = d\Upsilon/dx and elements of the matrix [Wij] are given by

Wii = kB T / ci + kB T / cA - 2\Omega b Ai - 2\Upsilon(\Omega gb Ai - \Omega b Ai), i=B,C, (16a)

Wij = Wji = kB T / cA + \Omega b ij + \Upsilon(\Omega gb ij - \Omega b ij), i,j=B,C, j \neq i. (16b)

Also, using the expression for the segregation energy Ei (see Eq. 4) elements of the vector {Ui} are given by

Ui = -\Delta G Ai + (\Omega gb Ai - \Omega b Ai)(1 - 2c) + (\Omega gb BC - \Omega b BC) c j, i,j=B,C, j \neq i. (17)

Finally, we plug Eq. 15 into Eq. 14 and re-arrange to yield the following nonlinear coupled system

{ \partial cB / \partial x } = -[Wij]-1 \Upsilon' {Ui} + V[Lij]-1 {ci - c\infty i}, (18)
{ \partial cC / \partial x }

where [···]-1 is the matrix inverse. Here, we note that [Wij] and {Ui} are functions of the concentrations, and, as a result, the right-hand side of Eq. 18 is only a function of the concentration fields of solutes B and C and the indicator function \Upsilon and its derivative. The first term on the right-hand side of Eq. 18 captures details about alloy thermodynamics and GB segregation energy, and the second term is dynamical related to the GB velocity and transport coefficients. It is straightforward to re-express the Onsager coefficients Lij in terms of the diffusion coefficients Dij as shown in Eq. 12. The last term on the right-hand side of Eq. 18 arises due to the imposed boundary conditions on the simulation domain. Equation 18 above is numerically solved in non-dimensional form using the finite difference method with a grid spacing \Delta x = 0.02\delta and a GB velocity V¯ in the range of 0 and 50 in increments of 0.1 for most cases explored in this work. Once the concentration profiles of solutes B and C are obtained, the non-dimensional GB solute drag P¯ is evaluated numerically using Eq. 8.
